`run_dev` calls the same function with `dev=true`, but the auto-increment branch ignores `dev`:

::code{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/android/dev.rs" lines="272-275"}

Every dev session therefore rewrites `tauri.properties` with a new code. The docs ask users to commit this file, so local development will keep producing noisy diffs and inflating the version number between releases. A build that later fails also leaves the bumped value behind. Consider incrementing only when `!dev`, and reusing the last value, or writing nothing new, for dev runs.

`saturating_add(1)` caps the value at `u32::MAX` (about 4.29e9), and nothing checks it against Android's 2,100,000,000 limit, which both `version_code` and the semver path enforce. A hand-edited or carried-over large value would produce an invalid code that only fails later, in Gradle or on the Play Console.

